Formatting commentary with Claude...

  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Risk appetite held up overnight — S&P and Dow futures pressed to fresh records on a strong AI-earnings pulse — but the real story is in the metals pit: gold ripped 2.6% higher to 4,261 and silver surged 3.4% after ADP showed private payrolls rising just 44k in July, the softest in six months and well under the 65k expected. Another sluggish jobs print stokes Fed-cut expectations and undercuts real yields, and with the Treasury's quarterly refunding confirming steady but heavy supply ($125bn, auctions unchanged "for at least several quarters"), the debasement-hedge bid in precious metals has fresh legs.

The long bond caught a bid on the growth signal even as the belly lagged ahead of next week's 3s, 10s and 30s. Energy is trading the geopolitics both ways: reports that a Strait of Hormuz deal could land as soon as today knocked WTI down 0.6% and dragged RBOB more than 1% lower, though Houthi strikes on a Saudi vessel in the Red Sea show the risk premium isn't dead — Brent held firm near 79.6. European TTF gas slid over 3%. Copper, meanwhile, sits just shy of record highs in New York on US tariff speculation and the Hormuz-driven risk bid, with Washington's export ban on scrap tungsten and black mass tightening the critical-minerals story further.

In ags, softs are the movers — arabica coffee up 1.6% at records on the US–Colombia tariff spat, cocoa and cotton firm — while corn and meal sagged. Watch Hormuz headlines, Friday-adjacent jobs follow-through, and the tariff decision hanging over copper.

